{"resourceIdentifier":"3743","resourceName":"RE.GREEN ATLANTIC FOREST REFORESTATION/RESTORATION PROJECT","description":"The Grouped Project aims to implement forest restoration activities throughout the Brazilian Atlantic Forest Biome (hereinafter referred to as the project zone), primarily by replacing exotic grasslands formerly used for cattle raising. The project zone encompasses 17 states within Brazilian territory and is located in climatic and edaphic conditions suitable for plant growth. It also includes a region of high value for biodiversity conservation.The initial objective is to restore approximately 88,400 hectares of forest cover within the project zone and to facilitate the subsequent sequestration and storage of carbon in various carbon pools, including aboveground biomass, belowground biomass, litter, dead wood, and soil organic carbon. Net greenhouse gas removals are estimated at approximately 48,346,689 tCO2e over 100 years, equivalent to the annual removal of 483,467 tCO2e. The reduction of greenhouse gases (GHGs), specifically CO2, through the project’s activities is achieved via biological sequestration through plant photosynthesis. This potential is enhanced by implementing a continuum of human-induced forest restoration activities, ranging from assisted natural regeneration to full planting. In some cases, the process includes the use of commercial timber species native to the region to trigger the initial restoration process. In the absence of the project, the project areas would remain dominated by degraded pastures with low carbon stocks as a result of unsustainable anthropogenic activities. The project’s first instance (PAI-001) encompasses 345 hectares, is located in the municipality of Eunápolis, Bahia, Brazil, and is expected to generate 194,712 tCO2 over 50 years, equivalent to 3,894 tCO2/ha/year. The project’s second instance (PAI-002) covers 1,278 hectares, is located in the municipality of Potiraguá, Bahia, Brazil, and is expected 